{
  "dom_id": "step-q348182",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Determine the tangent line approximation $L(x)$ of $\\sqrt{x}$ at $x=36.$\n\n$L(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=36,$ which gives\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(36)+{f}^{′}(36)(x-36).\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(36)$ and ${f}^{′}(36)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf(36)=\\sqrt{36}=6,\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(36)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{36}}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 6}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(36)+{f}^{′}(36)(x-36) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12}(x-36) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12}x-3 \\\\  & =6-3+\\frac{1}{12}x \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{12}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
